WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.040
Gorr the God Butcher is granted a wish for&nbsp;
literally anything and he doesn't think to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.260
save his daughter and himself? His choice isn’t&nbsp;
the only thing in the MCU that has us confused.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:15.120
In "Shang-Chi and the Ten Rings,"&nbsp;
Katy is a regular human with no superpowers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.040
no combat training, and no experience with&nbsp;
the supernatural. Her relative normalcy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.900
frankly, is what makes her endearing.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.360
But after she spends little more than a day&nbsp;
practicing archery with Guang Bo in Ta Lo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.720
she displays Hawkeye-level skills&nbsp;
in battle. Which would have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.920
a little easier to forgive if the&nbsp;
film's entire climax didn't hinge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.940
on Katy making a one-in-a-million shot&nbsp;
that nails the bad guy in the throat.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.820
After the movie, Marvel tried&nbsp;
to explain this in the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.360
with "Shang-Chi" #7 revealing&nbsp;
that when people go to Ta Lo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.700
they’re given special powers, including&nbsp;
super-archery. Nice try, Marvel. Nice try.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.960
It was awesome to see Yelena grace&nbsp;
the screen in the "Hawkeye" TV series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.280
but the reason for her appearance was quite&nbsp;
flimsy. It all came down to a misunderstanding;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.720
if the characters would have stopped hitting each&nbsp;
other in the face and simply had a conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.680
it could have been sorted out in two&nbsp;
minutes rather than across six episodes.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:14.100
Which was proven when they finally had a&nbsp;
conversation, and sorted it out in two minutes.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.360
"I'm sorry."

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.760
Supposedly, Valentina made Yelena&nbsp;
think Hawkeye was responsible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.900
the death of Black Widow. So Yelena spent&nbsp;
the series trying to assassinate Hawkeye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.980
only to accept much, much&nbsp;
later that it wasn't his fault.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.020
What's weird is that the events of "Avengers:&nbsp;
Endgame" appear to be known to the general public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.220
as Kamala Khan was able to describe&nbsp;
the fight pretty much play-by-play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:43.020
in “Ms. Marvel.” So why doesn’t&nbsp;
Yelena, a highly trained superspy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.940
know what everyone else in the world&nbsp;
knows? It just doesn't make any sense.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.580
At the end of "WandaVision," Wanda defeats&nbsp;
Agatha by casting runes on the Hex barrier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:58.140
to prevent Agatha from using her magic. So&nbsp;
long as Agatha is inside the circle of runes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:02.760
only Wanda can cast spells. However,&nbsp;
when Wanda allows the Hex to collapse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:08.580
Agatha should be able to access her powers&nbsp;
again. Only, she seemingly can’t. Why?

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:14.460
ScreenCrush theorizes that it doesn't matter if&nbsp;
Agatha is outside the Hex, because Wanda cast a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:19.740
spell on her while she was still inside the Hex to&nbsp;
trap her inside the mind of "Agnes." Once Agatha&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:25.980
becomes Agnes, argues the site, she has no free&nbsp;
will and presumably can't cast any spells. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:30.960
that explanation may not hold up to scrutiny,&nbsp;
because of what Agatha had previously told Wanda

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.520
"My thoughts are not available to&nbsp;
you, toots. They never, ever were."

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:41.400
If that’s true, then only Wanda's runes seem&nbsp;
to have had any real effect on Agatha. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.180
when she returns in "Agatha: Coven of&nbsp;
Chaos" we’ll get the answer as to why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.540
she couldn’t break free of Wanda’s&nbsp;
spell. But don’t hold your breath.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:53.040
In the first episode of "What If...?" the&nbsp;
Red Skull uses the Tesseract to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.360
a portal in hopes of unleashing a monster.&nbsp;
Captain Carter manages to stop the creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.320
but not before passing through the portal and&nbsp;
getting stranded in the twenty-first century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.060
However, there's one small problem:&nbsp;
the Tesseract doesn't work that way.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.540
Canonically, the Tesseract has been&nbsp;
established as something that can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:16.140
used to travel through space, not time — that's&nbsp;
why it's called the Space Stone. Yet somehow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:21.540
Captain Carter hurtles through space and&nbsp;
time, ending up almost 70 years in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:26.040
future. If the Space Stone could actually&nbsp;
allow the wielder to travel through time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:31.140
then that would have changed everything.&nbsp;
For instance, Thanos would have been much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:35.520
more powerful when he held only two Infinity&nbsp;
Stones in the beginning of "Infinity War."

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:39.840
But hey, the series is called "What&nbsp;
If...?" after all. Maybe the Infinity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.680
Stones operate according to different&nbsp;
rules in Captain Carter's universe?

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.440
The world was in jeopardy countless&nbsp;
times throughout Phase 4 of the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.660
ranging from a Celestial's emergence to the&nbsp;
Noor Dimension nearly obliterating Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.660 --> 00:04:01.440
and more. Throughout all this, where were the&nbsp;
Avengers? Even if the team itself is disbanded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.120
you'd think individuals like Dr. Strange&nbsp;
or Wong would have noticed Wanda's Chaos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:10.440
Magic at Westview, or Rhodey would've lent&nbsp;
Sam Wilson a hand after seeing that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.940
Flag Smashers were a real threat. But nope,&nbsp;
apparently they were taking some time off.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:18.480
Of course, if the Avengers showed&nbsp;
up every time there was a threat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.560
there wouldn’t be much for heroes to do in&nbsp;
their own solo films and shows. Because of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.120
the world was in danger plenty of times&nbsp;
before Phase 4 without the Avengers showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:30.900
up — just look at "Thor: The Dark World"&nbsp;
and "Captain America: The Winter Soldier."

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:35.220
But that's the explanation in our&nbsp;
universe. In the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:39.120
Universe? It might be nice if the MCU&nbsp;
acknowledged that the Avengers were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:41.640
at least paying attention to all&nbsp;
the crap happening in their world.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.660
Sometimes, by trying to resolve one plot hole,&nbsp;
you create another. Case in point: "Eternals"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:52.740
trying to explain why none of them tried to&nbsp;
stop Thanos from snapping half the universe.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.460
"We were instructed not to interfere in any human&nbsp;
conflicts unless the Deviants were involved."

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:02.040
But attempting to offer an explanation for the&nbsp;
absence of the Eternals only ended up raising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:07.740
more questions. The non-intervention policy of&nbsp;
the Eternals comes from their Celestial masters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:12.840
All they cared about was incubating their new&nbsp;
baby Celestial in the heart of Earth’s core.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:17.460
Here’s the issue: in order to do that,&nbsp;
the Celestials need the population of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:21.540
Earth to reach a certain point —&nbsp;
so having half of humanity snapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:26.160
out of existence would be a major problem.&nbsp;
Not to mention the fact that Thanos might,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.920
you know, also snap half of the Celestials out of&nbsp;
existence too? Which is also counter-productive.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:35.820
Maybe it would have been better if&nbsp;
the film didn’t mention Thanos at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:39.600
because now that we’re thinking&nbsp;
about it, it doesn’t make any sense.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.460
"Dr. Strange in the Multiverse of&nbsp;
Madness" has madness right in the title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.820
so it's easy to forgive any details&nbsp;
that don't make sense. However, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:52.380
is one plot hole from "Multiverse of Madness"&nbsp;
that's a little distracting: where is Vision?

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:55.740
For the entire movie, Wanda&nbsp;
seems to forget about Vision;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:00.060
instead she is only concerned with finding&nbsp;
Billy and Tommy from an alternate universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:06.120
Once Wanda finds her kids on Earth-838, Vision&nbsp;
is never shown on-screen. Even in the climax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:11.820
when Billy and Tommy fear for their lives from the&nbsp;
Scarlet Witch, Wanda-838 rushes to their rescue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:17.220
while Vision is nowhere to be found&nbsp;
-- not even the Vision from Earth-838.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:21.960
There could be reasons why Vision wasn’t in&nbsp;
this movie besides the prosaic possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:25.800
that actor Paul Bettany had a scheduling&nbsp;
conflict. But given that Wanda’s entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:30.720
arc in the MCU was intertwined with&nbsp;
Vision, it was weird and disappointing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.160
that he was barely even mentioned in "Dr.&nbsp;
Strange in the Multiverse of Madness.”

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.760
Given they are planet-destroying, god-like&nbsp;
beings who shaped the universe's development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:47.520
you’d think someone would notice the Celestials.&nbsp;
But nobody does unless the plot directs it.&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.240
During Phase 3's "Guardians of&nbsp;
the Galaxy, Vol. 2," for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:57.300
the Celestial Ego almost consumed the entire&nbsp;
Earth without the Avengers even noticing. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.780
this world-threatening event is never&nbsp;
acknowledged elsewhere in the MCU.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:05.460
Then there’s "Eternals," which is even&nbsp;
worse. Although the Eternals stopped the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:09.120
new Celestial from fully emerging,&nbsp;
the parts of the Celestial that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:14.100
already sprouted from the planet's surface&nbsp;
would have still done cataclysmic damage to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:19.260
Earth. A colossal hand rising from the&nbsp;
Earth's mantle and bursting through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:24.240
ocean floor would be plenty sufficient&nbsp;
to wipe out almost all life on Earth.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:30.000
Not only does the movie never explore these&nbsp;
consequences, but nobody seems to even notice the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.940
gigantic Celestial protruding from the ocean. The&nbsp;
barely-averted apocalypse has yet to be mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:41.640
again, aside from a split-second glimpse of a&nbsp;
news headline on She-Hulk’s laptop in "She-Hulk."

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:44.580
The biggest question about the Celestials:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:49.020
if nobody in the MCU cares about&nbsp;
anything they do, why should we?

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:55.020
The climax of "Thor: Love and Thunder" gives&nbsp;
the villain Gorr a satisfying redemption.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.020 --> 00:08:00.480
Rather than choosing to get revenge on&nbsp;
Thor and the other gods, Gorr instead asks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:05.040
Eternity to give his dead daughter a&nbsp;
second chance at life. It’s very moving.

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.740
Unfortunately, though, it&nbsp;
doesn't entirely make sense.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:12.300
The movie acts as though Gorr only&nbsp;
has two choices: wish to destroy all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:16.380
the gods as he originally intended or&nbsp;
wish his daughter back to life — even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:19.980
though he is dying from a curse and&nbsp;
won't be able to take care of her.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:25.980
But as far as the movie seems to explain, Gorr&nbsp;
can wish for just about anything. The rules behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:30.240
Eternity are hazy. It seems like, if&nbsp;
Gorr phrased his wish more carefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.380
he wouldn't need to choose between saving&nbsp;
his own life and his daughter's. For one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:38.280
Gorr could simply wish to be reunited&nbsp;
with his daughter. Or he could wish that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:41.880
the drought that wiped out his people never&nbsp;
happened. That would mean his daughter would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:45.240
still be alive, and he would never&nbsp;
have sworn vengeance on the gods.

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:48.720
Ultimately, the film's emotionally&nbsp;
bittersweet ending would've felt more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.580
earned if it had laid down some rules to the&nbsp;
wishes beforehand so any of it made sense.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:59.880
Dr. Strange has been repeatedly depicted as&nbsp;
taking his duties with the utmost of fidelity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:04.800
even refusing to give up the Time Stone in&nbsp;
"Infinity War" despite it placing a target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:10.140
on his back. So it was surprising when Dr.&nbsp;
Strange agreed to cast a risky spell for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:14.700
Peter in "No Way Home," especially because&nbsp;
he only seems to be doing it to spite Wong.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:19.740
Of course, the spell backfired, so instead of&nbsp;
making everybody forget that Peter Parker was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:24.240
Spider-Man, it summoned everybody who knew&nbsp;
Spider-Man's secret identity, in this and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.980
other alternate universes. The whole disaster&nbsp;
could have been avoided if Peter and Strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:34.020
had simply taken a moment to discuss their plan&nbsp;
before casting any spells. Come to think of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:38.700
it might have been a lot more simple to cast a&nbsp;
spell to make people forget Mysterio instead.

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:43.260
And even if Strange's spell hadn't blown up&nbsp;
his face, it still wouldn't have done much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:48.360
good. Strange emphasized that his spell wouldn't&nbsp;
change history; it would just alter everyone's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:52.260
memories. But erasing memories wouldn't&nbsp;
be enough. There would still be plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:56.940
evidence about Spider-Man's secret identity that&nbsp;
would be impossible to ignore. The spell would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:02.700
be rendered useless as soon as somebody Googled&nbsp;
Spider-Man or Mysterio, or read an old newspaper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:08.100
or watched an old news program, simply because the&nbsp;
incriminating physical evidence would still exist.

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:13.080
Doctor Strange does ultimately cast a&nbsp;
spell that somehow succeeds in erasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:18.300
Peter’s secret from the public record. Maybe&nbsp;
he should have used that spell the first time.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:22.500
Despite opinions to the contrary,&nbsp;
the final episode of "She-Hulk:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:27.300
Attorney-at-Law" made sense. Jen smashing through&nbsp;
the fourth wall was consistent with the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:31.320
and it was fun that Jen called out the&nbsp;
cliche of the bad guy turning into a Hulk.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:34.080
"This can't possibly be&nbsp;
where this season was going!"

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:38.340
The only problem was that the&nbsp;
season's hasty resolution left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:41.760
some confusion about how much of&nbsp;
what the show depicted was real.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:45.240
Since Todd didn't turn into a&nbsp;
Hulk in Jen's alternate ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:51.240
then what exactly happened? Did Jen just call&nbsp;
the cops, and Todd was arrested without even a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:55.980
struggle? Would the cops even believe Jen, after&nbsp;
she'd lost control at the gala? For that matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:00.720
what exactly was Todd's master&nbsp;
plan? To simply defame She-Hulk?

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.960
Also, did the scenes with&nbsp;
Jen dating Josh never happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:08.460
or just the part where he steals her blood?&nbsp;
If Jen wrote Josh out of the show completely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:13.920
that would have huge repercussions — namely, the&nbsp;
crucial scene where Jen attends Emil Blonsky's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:18.840
retreat would have never happened. It's not such&nbsp;
a stretch to imagine Josh stringing Jen along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:24.000
just so he could film Jen sleeping with him and&nbsp;
release it at the gala to humiliate Jen. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:29.280
it would have been nice to have the final episode&nbsp;
at least offer clarity on which parts were real.

